// `let (a, b) = f()` used to compile and bind nothing: the let handler reads a
// single identifier for the name, so the `(` was taken as the name and the
// elements never existed. The statement produced no diagnostic, and you only
// found out at the first use of one of the names.
//
// Only the call form is rejected. `let (a, b) = (x, y)` binds in parallel and
// is supported -- see tests/conformance/stage1/pass/let_tuple_literal.jda. The
// call form needs multi-value return, and OP_RET carries one operand, so a
// function returning several values returns a struct.
fn f() -> i64 { ret 7 }

fn main() {
    let (a, b) = f()
    print("{a}\n")
}
